`,"• Level 1 (Condition Report) — basic, not recommended for most",`
`,"• Level 2 (HomeBuyer Report) — most common, £400–£800",`
`,"• Level 3 (Building Survey) — older/unusual properties, £600–£1,500",`
`,"A poor survey result can justify renegotiating the price."]}),s.jsx(Va,{n:5,title:"Exchange of contracts",children:"This is the legally binding stage. You pay your deposit (typically 5–10% of purchase price) to the solicitor. If you withdraw after exchange, you lose your deposit. The seller must also complete or they face penalties."}),s.jsx(Va,{n:6,title:"Completion",children:"The remaining funds (from your mortgage + remaining deposit) are transferred to the seller's solicitor. You receive the keys. Your solicitor pays Stamp Duty and registers you at Land Registry."}),s.jsx(VZ,{children:"After completion, your solicitor will register the title with HMLR. This can take weeks to months — this is normal. You'll receive an official title register once done."})]}),e==="rental"&&s.jsxs(ra,{title:"Renting Out Your Property — Landlord Obligations",children:[s.jsx(xe,{title:"EPC (Energy Performance Certificate)",children:"Your property must have an EPC rating of E or above to let it legally. Band A is best, G is worst. New build? Likely A or B. Older home? May need insulation/boiler upgrades. Fines up to £30,000 for non-compliance."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Gas Safety Certificate",children:"Annual inspection by a Gas Safe registered engineer. Certificate must be provided to tenants. Required every 12 months. Keep records for 2 years."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Electrical Safety Certificate (EICR)",children:"Required every 5 years for all private rentals. Must be conducted by a qualified electrician. Copy provided to tenants within 28 days of inspection."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Smoke & CO Alarms",children:"Smoke alarm required on every floor. CO alarm required in any room with a solid fuel appliance (e.g. wood burner). Interlinked alarms now required in Scotland."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Tenancy Deposit Protection",children:"Deposits must be protected in a government-approved scheme within 30 days of receipt: TDS (Tenancy Deposit Service), DPS (Deposit Protection Service), or MyDeposits. Failure = 1–3× deposit penalty plus inability to serve valid Section 21 notice."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Right to Rent Checks",children:"You must check that all adult tenants have the legal right to rent in England. Carry out checks before tenancy starts, copy and retain documents."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"HMO Licensing",children:"If you let to 5 or more unrelated tenants sharing facilities, you likely need an HMO (House in Multiple Occupation) licence from the local council. Some councils require licensing for smaller HMOs — check locally."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Rent-a-Room Scheme",children:"If you live in the property and rent a room, you can earn up to £7,500/year tax-free. Income above this is taxable. You automatically qualify — no need to register. Applies to furnished rooms only."})]}),e==="airbnb"&&s.jsxs(ra,{title:"Short-Term Lets & Airbnb (England)",children:[s.jsx(xe,{title:"90-Day Rule (London Only)",children:"In Greater London, you can short-let a property for up to 90 nights per calendar year without planning permission. Exceeding this requires planning consent from your local authority. Outside London, no national day limit exists but local councils may have rules."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Mortgage Permission",children:"Most standard residential mortgages do NOT permit short-term letting. Check your mortgage terms. A specialist buy-to-let or holiday let mortgage may be required. Letting without permission is a mortgage breach and could trigger repayment demands."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Leasehold Properties",children:"If your property is leasehold (most flats), your lease may prohibit short-term letting. Check your lease carefully — violation can result in forfeiture of the lease."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Insurance",children:"Standard home insurance doesn't cover short-term letting. You need specialist short-term let or holiday let insurance covering public liability, damage, and lost income."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Tax on Short-Term Let Income",children:"Furnished Holiday Let (FHL) rules may apply if you let for 210+ days/year and it's available 210+ days/year. FHL status allows capital allowances. Otherwise, income is taxed as property income. The £1,000 property allowance and Rent-a-Room (£7,500) can reduce liability if conditions are met."}),s.jsx(xe,{title:"Council Tax vs Business Rates",children:"Properties let for more than 140 days/year in England may switch from council tax to business rates. Small Business Rate Relief may apply — check with your local authority."})]}),e==="tax"&&s.jsxs(ra,{title:"Tax Obligations for UK Property Investors",children:[s.jsx(xe,{title:"Self-Assessment Registration",children:"If your rental income exceeds £1,000/year (the property allowance), you must register for Self-Assessment with HMRC and file a tax return annually. Deadline: 31 January following the tax year end (5 April)."}),s.jsxs(xe,{title:"Income Tax on Rental Income",children:["Rental profit (income minus allowable expenses) is added to your other income and taxed at your marginal rate:",`
`,"• Basic rate: 20% (up to £50,270)",`
